The '''2026 Balboa data center protests''' were a series of nationwide protests held across [[Balboa]] from 18 to 27 August 2026, triggered by the commencement of construction of a [[Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses]] (NTE) data center facility near [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] in the [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] highlands. The protests were orchestrated by a coalition of leftist political parties, indigenous rights organisations, trade unions, and environmental groups, and represented the most significant wave of social unrest in Balboa since the [[Adonian pandemic]] lockdowns of 2020–2022.

The protests began on 18 August 2026, when NTE formally broke ground on the Sant Jorge facility — a site that had been the subject of sustained opposition from [[Birú people|indigenous communities]] since its proposal in early 2026. Within hours of the groundbreaking, protesters gathered at the construction site and in cities across the country, including [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Sant Bart]], [[Portblanc]], and [[Ciudad Obrera]]. The demonstrations rapidly expanded over the following days, with leftist groups coordinating nationwide actions under a shared platform opposing data center construction on indigenous lands, the fossil fuel energy infrastructure required to power the facilities, and the broader prioritisation of digital infrastructure over social and environmental concerns.

Police were deployed to disperse protesters at multiple sites, with the largest confrontations occurring at the Sant Jorge construction site and in Sant Cristòfor. The protests lasted nine days, ending on 27 August. Construction at the Sant Jorge site was halted pending a government review.

== Background ==

{{main|Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses}}

NTE, founded in 2024 and listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 2025, had rapidly expanded its data center operations across Balboa, with operational facilities near [[Central Nuclear de Sant Feliu|Sant Feliu]] in [[Jala Province|Jala]] and in [[Portblanc]], and additional facilities under construction in [[Potocsí]] and [[Tapuya]]. The company's expansion had drawn criticism from the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB) and environmental groups over its energy consumption, its funding of a new [[natural gas]]-fired power station in Portblanc, and the environmental and social impact of its facilities.

The proposed Sant Jorge facility, announced in early 2026, drew immediate opposition from [[Birú people|Birú]] communities in the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca, who raised concerns about land use, environmental impact, and the disruption of cultural sites. The opposition was compounded by the region's history of conflict over infrastructure development, particularly the construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] dam in the 1970s, which had required the relocation of the Birú village of [[Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana|Vila de Llevant]] and remained a source of grievance among indigenous communities.

Prior to the groundbreaking, the Sant Jorge facility was reported to be in the planning phase, with no construction permits issued. The commencement of construction on 18 August came as a surprise to opposition groups and triggered the immediate protests.

== Protests ==

The protests began on 18 August 2026, hours after NTE formally broke ground at the Sant Jorge site. Demonstrators gathered at the construction site, blocking access roads and preventing construction equipment from operating. Simultaneous demonstrations were held in [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Sant Bart]], [[Portblanc]], and other cities, coordinated by a coalition of leftist organisations led by the [[Moviment del poble Birú]] (MPB) and the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB).

Over the following days, the protests expanded to include trade union members, students, indigenous rights activists, and environmental organisations. The demonstrators' demands centred on three issues: the construction of data centers on or near indigenous lands without community consent, the fossil fuel energy infrastructure required to power the facilities, and the government's perceived prioritisation of private digital infrastructure over social and environmental concerns.

Police were deployed to disperse protesters at multiple locations. The largest confrontations occurred at the Sant Jorge construction site, where police attempted to clear roadblocks and restore access for NTE construction crews, and in Sant Cristòfor, where demonstrations outside government buildings led to clashes with riot police. Reports of injuries and arrests were documented across multiple protest sites.

The protests continued for nine days, with daily demonstrations and roadblocks maintained at the Sant Jorge site and in major cities. On 27 August, the government announced that construction at the Sant Jorge site would be halted pending a review of the project's environmental and social impact, and the protests subsided.

== Aftermath ==

The halt in construction at the Sant Jorge site represented a significant victory for the protest coalition and intensified the national debate over data center regulation in Balboa. The protests highlighted the tension between the government's push for digital infrastructure development and the rights of indigenous communities, a dynamic that had previously surfaced in connection with hydroelectric dam construction in the Tramuntana highlands.

The protests prompted calls from the FSB and allied organisations for a moratorium on new data center construction, stricter environmental impact assessment requirements, and mandatory consultation with indigenous communities for projects on or near traditional lands. The Balboan government announced a review of the regulatory framework for data center construction, though no formal legislative changes had been enacted as of late August 2026.

NTE's stock price declined significantly during the protests, reflecting investor uncertainty about the company's expansion plans. The company issued a statement acknowledging the concerns raised and committing to engage with indigenous communities, while maintaining that the Sant Jorge site had been selected in compliance with all applicable regulations.

The protests also drew attention to the history of private security deployments at protest sites in the Tramuntana highlands. The [[2021 Carretera Gran Presa del Nord protest]], at which [[Hernandez Lee]] was kidnapped and killed by a [[Nivell de Seguretat]] guard, was cited by MPB activists as evidence of the dangers of private security at indigenous rights demonstrations. The 2021 case and the subsequent civil lawsuit — in which Nivell de Seguretat was found not liable — were invoked as rallying points during the 2026 protests.

